.TH FBV 1 "2026-10-30" "fbv-ng 2.0.0"
.SH NAME
fbv \- framebuffer image viewer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fbv
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIimage\fR...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fbv
shows BMP, PNG, JPEG and GIF images, including animated GIFs, on a Linux
framebuffer console. The formats available depend on the build.
.PP
It draws through the framebuffer device, so the kernel must provide one: a
DRM/KMS driver with fbdev emulation (CONFIG_DRM_FBDEV_EMULATION and
CONFIG_FB_DEVICE) or a classic fbdev driver. The user needs write access to the
device, usually through the video group.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-alpha
Blend the image with the screen using its alpha channel.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-noclear
Do not clear the screen before and after displaying the image.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-unhide
Do not hide the cursor while displaying the image.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-noinfo
Do not show image information.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-shrink
Shrink the image to fit onto the screen if necessary.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-colorshrink
Shrink with a color averaging filter.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-enlarge
Enlarge the image to fit the whole screen if necessary.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-widthonly
When fitting, fit horizontally only.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-heightonly
When fitting, fit vertically only.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-smartfit " \fIpercent\fR"
Cover the whole screen if less than \fIpercent\fR of the image is cut off.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-ignore\-aspect
Ignore the image aspect while resizing.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-delay " \fIdelay\fR"
Slideshow: show each image for \fIdelay\fR tenths of a second. A key press
stops the timer for the current image.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-imagename " \fInames\fR"
Names shown in the image information, separated by `^' for several files.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-orientation " \fIo\fR"
Initial rotation: 0 none, 1 90 degrees, 2 180 degrees, 3 270 degrees.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-skiptty
Do not read keys: show the images and continue, playing animations to their
end and waiting for the slideshow delay.
.TP
.BR \-C ", " \-\-control " \fIpath\fR"
Accept commands on the Unix socket \fIpath\fR, see CONTROL. The viewer then
keeps running until it gets \fBquit\fR. The socket is only accessible to its
owner, and a socket another fbv still serves is not taken over.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-graphics
Switch the active console to graphics mode while running, so the kernel draws
no text or cursor over the image.
.SH KEYS
.TS
l l.
r	Redraw the image
< or ,	Previous image
> or .	Next image
space, enter	Next image
a, d, w, x	Scroll the image (cursor keys also do that)
f	Toggle shrinking
k	Toggle shrinking quality
e	Toggle enlarging
l	Toggle fitting the image horizontally
t	Toggle fitting the image vertically
i	Toggle respecting the image aspect
+, -, 0	Increase, decrease and reset zoom
n	Rotate the image 90 degrees left
m	Rotate the image 90 degrees right
p	Disable all transformations
h	Help and image information
q	Quit
.TE
.SH ANIMATION
Animated GIFs play with their frame delays, delays below 20 ms count as
100 ms. The loop count stored in the file is honoured; a file without one
plays once. Without a terminal on standard input, the viewer moves to the
next image when the animation ends.
.SH CONTROL
Each line sent to the control socket is one command, answered by one line:
\fBok\fR, possibly followed by values, or \fBerror\fR and a reason.
.TP
.B ping
Check that the viewer is alive.
.TP
.BR pause ", " resume
Stop and restart the animation clock. \fBresume\fR also returns to free
playback after \fBframe\fR or \fBprogress\fR.
.TP
.BI frame " n"
Show frame \fIn\fR of the animation, counting from 0, and hold it.
.TP
.BI progress " percent"
Move to the frame matching \fIpercent\fR (0 to 100, decimals allowed) of
the animation and hold it there. Forward moves step through the frames at the
animation's own pace, backward moves jump.
.TP
.BI load " file"
Show another image or animation; on error the current one stays.
.TP
.B status
Reply \fBok frame=\fIn\fB frames=\fIcount\fB paused=\fI0|1\fR.
.TP
.BR quit " [" retain ]
Exit, leaving the image on the framebuffer. With \fBretain\fR the console
stays in graphics mode, for a program taking over the display.
.PP
For example, with socat:
.PP
.nf
fbv -y -c -i -g --control /run/fbv.sock boot.gif &
echo "progress 40" | socat - UNIX-CONNECT:/run/fbv.sock
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FRAMEBUFFER
Framebuffer device, /dev/fb0 when unset.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on errors, 128 plus the signal number when interrupted.
.SH AUTHORS
fbv by Mateusz Golicz and Tomasz Sterna; fbv-ng by Angelo Compagnucci.
